schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Gathering Formats for Community Building
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A typology of gathering containers (informal, educational,
    decision-making, celebration) chosen deliberately to match a
    community's developmental stage, on the premise that the format shapes
    the outcome more than the topic does.
  source_traditions:
    - Priya Parker's The Art of Gathering (intentional gathering design)
    - agile retrospective practice — Norm Kerth's Project Retrospectives (2001) and its Prime Directive
    - sociocracy's consent-based decision-making (Sociocracy For All)
    - The World Café (Juanita Brown) and Open Space Technology (Harrison Owen) — participant-driven large-group formats
    - Liberating Structures — a menu of facilitation microstructures
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Applied as a stage-matched menu (pre-formation, early formation, active
    operations, growth, renewal) for regen local-node organizers, with a
    sustainability constraint (weekly/monthly/quarterly rhythm) layered on
    top of the format choice.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - facilitation microstructures (Liberating Structures) as a lighter-weight adjacent toolkit to full gathering formats
    - consent-based decision-making as both an offline meeting practice and a rehearsal for on-chain governance tools (Snapshot, multisig)
  important_distinctions:
    - consent (no remaining objections — Does anyone object / Can you live with this) vs consensus (unanimous enthusiasm)
    - a retrospective (structured look-back on a project/period) vs a town hall (forward-looking proposal decision)
    - informal relationship-building gatherings (foundation layer) vs formal decision-making gatherings (built on top of that foundation)
  tensions:
    - the guide's own warning against turning a relationship-building meal into a decision-making meeting ("while we're all here, let's just quickly...")
    - predictability/rhythm vs the temptation to run an ambitious, high-effort calendar
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Presenting a five-stage node lifecycle (pre-formation to renewal) as a
    clean linear progression risks flattening the reality that real
    communities cycle between stages non-linearly (e.g., renewal needs can
    arise mid-"active operations"); the guide does gesture at this by
    listing renewal as its own recurring stage rather than a one-time end
    point.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/types-of-gatherings.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  source_lineage: types-of-gatherings.md
